1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,457
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,457 --> 00:00:07,576
This is chapter twelve: Java 14 to 17.

3
00:00:08,026 --> 00:00:16,218
Four features arrived together and were designed to compose: switch expressions, text blocks, records, and sealed types.

4
00:00:16,218 --> 00:00:18,198
Each one removes boilerplate.

5
00:00:18,198 --> 00:00:24,751
Together they give Java algebraic data types, which is what makes pattern matching worth having.

6
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:34,162
A value object in Java 8 is a page of mechanical code: fields, a constructor, getters, equals, hashCode and toString.

7
00:00:34,162 --> 00:00:41,509
Every line can be got wrong, and when you add a field, equals and hashCode quietly drift out of sync.

8
00:00:41,509 --> 00:00:45,001
That's a standing source of bugs in collections.

9
00:00:45,451 --> 00:00:47,381
In Java 16, it's one line.

10
00:00:47,381 --> 00:00:58,891
A record generates private final fields, a constructor, accessors, and equals, hashCode and toString, all derived from the components, so they can't drift.

11
00:00:59,791 --> 00:01:04,868
Here's the through-line: make the compiler able to reason about your data.

12
00:01:04,868 --> 00:01:08,985
Switch expressions, in Java 14, make switch produce a value.

13
00:01:09,435 --> 00:01:13,489
Text blocks, in 15, give you readable multi-line strings.

14
00:01:13,939 --> 00:01:18,781
Records, in 16, tell the compiler: these components are the whole state.

15
00:01:19,231 --> 00:01:23,455
And sealed types, in 17, tell it: these are all the cases.

16
00:01:23,905 --> 00:01:30,031
Combine a sealed interface with record implementations and you have an algebraic data type.

17
00:01:30,031 --> 00:01:37,772
An exhaustive switch over it needs no default, and adding a new case becomes a compile error everywhere it matters.

18
00:01:38,672 --> 00:01:42,104
The feature people miss is the compact constructor.

19
00:01:42,104 --> 00:01:48,228
It has no parameter list, runs before the fields are assigned, and works on the parameters.

20
00:01:48,228 --> 00:01:53,073
So you validate there, and you can normalise by reassigning a parameter.

21
00:01:53,073 --> 00:01:56,976
Here, the amount is always stored with two decimal places.

22
00:01:57,426 --> 00:02:02,825
And the single most common record bug: records are only shallowly immutable.

23
00:02:02,825 --> 00:02:07,584
If a component is a list, whoever passed it in can still change it.

24
00:02:07,584 --> 00:02:12,059
Always defensively copy collections in the compact constructor.

25
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:16,818
List dot copyOf also rejects nulls, which is usually what you want.

26
00:02:17,718 --> 00:02:19,285
Here's the combination.

27
00:02:19,285 --> 00:02:23,849
OrderState is a sealed interface that permits exactly four records.

28
00:02:23,849 --> 00:02:26,574
Each state carries exactly its own data.

29
00:02:26,574 --> 00:02:31,614
A pending order has no payment reference, because it doesn't have one yet.

30
00:02:31,614 --> 00:02:37,473
That removes a whole class of bugs: nullable fields that are only sometimes filled in.

31
00:02:37,923 --> 00:02:44,123
Now a switch over the state needs no default, because the compiler knows the set is closed.

32
00:02:44,123 --> 00:02:50,801
Add a fifth state, and this method stops compiling, along with every other switch over OrderState.

33
00:02:50,801 --> 00:02:53,731
The change surfaces everywhere it needs to.

34
00:02:54,631 --> 00:02:59,167
Switch expressions are a different construct, not just nicer syntax.

35
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:03,569
The arrow form never falls through, so there's no forgotten break.

36
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:07,907
It's an expression, so it can initialise a final variable.

37
00:03:07,907 --> 00:03:13,939
The old statement form can't, because the compiler can't prove the variable gets assigned.

38
00:03:14,389 --> 00:03:18,490
On enums and sealed types, it's checked for exhaustiveness.

39
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:20,853
A missing case is a compile error.

40
00:03:21,303 --> 00:03:26,530
And when a branch needs several statements, use a block with yield to produce the value.

41
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:27,829
One warning.

42
00:03:27,829 --> 00:03:34,196
Adding a default branch to an enum or sealed switch switches off the exhaustiveness check.

43
00:03:34,196 --> 00:03:37,239
A new constant silently falls into default.

44
00:03:37,239 --> 00:03:40,847
If you want the compiler's help, leave default out.

45
00:03:41,747 --> 00:03:44,534
Text blocks end the quote-counting.

46
00:03:44,534 --> 00:03:48,595
A SQL query is just the SQL, between triple quotes.

47
00:03:48,595 --> 00:03:50,984
No plus signs, no backslash n.

48
00:03:51,434 --> 00:03:57,995
The common leading indentation is stripped automatically, measured against the closing delimiter.

49
00:03:57,995 --> 00:04:00,971
So moving that delimiter changes the string.

50
00:04:00,971 --> 00:04:05,706
Harmless for SQL, but not for anything whitespace-sensitive like YAML.

51
00:04:06,156 --> 00:04:13,815
A backslash at the end of a line joins it to the next, and backslash s keeps a trailing space that would otherwise be removed.

52
00:04:14,265 --> 00:04:17,327
And text blocks are literals, not templates.

53
00:04:17,327 --> 00:04:19,067
There's no interpolation.

54
00:04:19,067 --> 00:04:20,806
Use the formatted method.

55
00:04:20,806 --> 00:04:24,077
String templates were proposed, then withdrawn.

56
00:04:24,077 --> 00:04:25,678
That's chapter fifteen.

57
00:04:26,578 --> 00:04:28,886
Records aren't always right.

58
00:04:28,886 --> 00:04:31,358
Never use one as a JPA entity.

59
00:04:31,358 --> 00:04:39,271
Entities have identity, mutable state, and lifecycle, and need a no-arg constructor and proxies.

60
00:04:39,271 --> 00:04:45,287
Records work great as JPA projections and API DTOs, just not as entities.

61
00:04:45,737 --> 00:04:49,212
Don't use one where identity matters more than value.

62
00:04:49,212 --> 00:04:52,884
Two connections to the same host aren't interchangeable.

63
00:04:53,334 --> 00:04:56,742
And don't let a record grow to fifteen components.

64
00:04:56,742 --> 00:04:59,810
Positional construction stops being readable.

65
00:04:59,810 --> 00:05:02,059
Use a builder, or split the type.

66
00:05:02,959 --> 00:05:09,431
Under the hood, a record's equals, hashCode and toString aren't ordinary generated bytecode.

67
00:05:09,431 --> 00:05:14,848
They're invokedynamic call sites, built from the component list at first use.

68
00:05:14,848 --> 00:05:19,983
So the class file stays small, and behaviour can't drift from the fields.

69
00:05:20,433 --> 00:05:27,295
Sealed types write a permitted subclasses attribute that the JVM enforces at class load time.

70
00:05:27,295 --> 00:05:33,788
It's a runtime guarantee, which is what lets the compiler treat exhaustiveness as sound.

71
00:05:34,238 --> 00:05:39,910
And an exhaustive switch still gets a hidden default that throws a MatchException.

72
00:05:39,910 --> 00:05:47,726
If someone adds a case and recompiles only the interface, your old switch fails loudly instead of returning null.

73
00:05:47,726 --> 00:05:52,499
That's also why libraries should be careful about sealing public API.

74
00:05:53,399 --> 00:05:54,688
Production rules.

75
00:05:54,688 --> 00:05:58,629
Use records for DTOs, value objects and projections.

76
00:05:58,629 --> 00:06:04,162
Validate in the compact constructor, and copy every collection component.

77
00:06:04,162 --> 00:06:08,255
Model closed domains as a sealed interface of records.

78
00:06:08,255 --> 00:06:11,741
Leave default out of sealed and enum switches.

79
00:06:11,741 --> 00:06:14,925
And use text blocks for SQL, JSON and XML.

80
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:20,732
Never use a record as a JPA entity, and never assume it's deeply immutable.

81
00:06:20,732 --> 00:06:24,090
Never add default to a switch you want checked.

82
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:28,519
Don't use non-sealed casually, since it reopens the hierarchy.

83
00:06:28,519 --> 00:06:29,876
Keep records small.

84
00:06:29,876 --> 00:06:34,233
And don't trust text-block indentation to survive a reformat.

85
00:06:35,133 --> 00:06:41,256
Java 14 made switch an expression, and gave us helpful NullPointerException messages.

86
00:06:41,706 --> 00:06:44,266
Java 15 finalised text blocks.

87
00:06:44,716 --> 00:06:49,367
Java 16 finalised records, along with instanceof patterns.

88
00:06:49,817 --> 00:06:55,193
Java 17 finalised sealed classes, the last piece before pattern matching.

89
00:06:55,643 --> 00:07:04,667
Java 21 and 22 delivered pattern matching for switch, record patterns, and the underscore for components you don't need.

90
00:07:05,117 --> 00:07:13,287
And Java 25 lets you run statements before calling super, so validation no longer needs static helper methods.

91
00:07:14,187 --> 00:07:14,789
To recap.

92
00:07:14,789 --> 00:07:21,212
Records are transparent carriers for immutable data, but remember to copy collection components.

93
00:07:21,212 --> 00:07:27,100
Sealed interfaces plus records give you closed domains the compiler checks exhaustively.

94
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:31,181
Switch expressions have no fall-through and can yield values.

95
00:07:31,181 --> 00:07:35,798
And text blocks strip indentation according to the closing delimiter.

96
00:07:36,248 --> 00:07:40,642
Next is chapter thirteen, pattern matching, where all of this pays off.

97
00:07:40,642 --> 00:07:41,880
Thanks for watching.
